<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Larova · French strings.

  A translation, not a source: never fix wording here. Fix the English in values/strings.xml and
  re-translate, or the two drift apart and nobody can tell which one is right. See
  docs/localization.md.

  **A model draft, not yet read by a native speaker.** docs/localization.md §5 names the three
  parts that must not ship on a machine draft — the help bar and contact sheet, the two view names,
  and the six templates — so those are where a review pass starts.

  Two decisions run through the file:

  · The reader is addressed with "vous" where an address form is unavoidable. The person on a
    caregiver screen is often not family, and the English almost never addresses anybody at all —
    infinitives ("Saisir le code") carry most of it.
  · Typographic apostrophes (’) throughout rather than ASCII ones, which keeps the file free of
    escaping questions and is correct French typography besides.
-->
